    "slug": "detroit-schools-expand-cash-incentive-program-to-boost-attendance",
    "summary": "Detroit Public Schools will pay students up to $100 weekly for full attendance in 2026-27, expanding a pilot program to middle schoolers. The initiative aims to reduce chronic absenteeism in the district.",
    "content": "Detroit Public Schools has announced an expansion of its attendance incentive program for the upcoming 2026-27 academic year. Under the initiative, high school students who maintain full attendance will receive $100 per week, while students in grades 6 through 8 will be eligible for $50 weekly. The payments are part of the district's broader effort to address persistent absenteeism, which has been linked to lower academic performance and higher dropout rates.\n\nThe program, known as \"Perfect Attendance Pays,\" was originally launched as a pilot targeting high school students. District officials say early results showed a measurable improvement in attendance among participating students, prompting the decision to broaden its reach. The expansion will now include middle school students, with funding allocated from the district's existing budget for student support services.\n\nDetroit has faced chronic absenteeism challenges for years, with a significant share of students missing 10% or more of school days. The problem was exacerbated by the pandemic and remote learning disruptions. By offering direct financial incentives, the district hopes to encourage consistent school attendance and address the root causes of truancy, such as economic hardship and lack of engagement.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora in Detroit, this program may be particularly relevant, as many families in the community have faced economic barriers and educational disruptions. The cash incentives could help alleviate financial pressures while promoting academic stability. Community leaders have expressed cautious optimism, emphasizing the need for complementary supports such as tutoring and mental health services.\n\nThe district plans to monitor the program's impact and adjust as needed. Similar incentive models in other U.S. cities have shown mixed results, with some studies suggesting that sustained engagement, rather than short-term rewards, is key to long-term success. Detroit's expansion will be watched closely as a case study in tackling absenteeism through direct financial support.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/08/22/mamulaha-school-ka-oo-ardayda-lacag-ku-siinaya-xaadirista-dugsiga-hoose-sare/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=mamulaha-school-ka-oo-ardayda-lacag-ku-siinaya-xaadirista-dugsiga-hoose-sare",
